@section(Physical supply) 
Land cover data is used to identify different land cover types spatially. These land cover types are assigned an IPCC Tier 1 carbon stock value @cite(IPCC2006) @cite(IPCC2013) @cite(IPCC2019) based on their type, geographic region and ecological zone (for forests)/climate zone (for non-forests) @cite(JRC2010) @cite(FAO2012). Forest (and some shrub) land cover classes are defined by continent, Global Ecological Zone (GEZ) @cite(FAO2012), management practices and age. Forest classes are stratified into Primary Forest, Secondary Old, Secondary Young, Plantation Old and Plantation Young, as per IPCC (2019) tables @cite(IPCC2019). The stratification methodology utilises global and regional datasets to identify each forest type, aligned with methodologies employed by Bourgoin et al. (2025) @cite(Bourgoin2025) and Hunka et al. (2024) @cite(Hunka2024). Datasets were selected based on geographic coverage, spatial and temporal resolution. The baseline year for the analysis is 2020. Therefore, all ‘young’ forest cells in 2020 are assigned an approximate age, which will be updated for further analysis years to ensure additional carbon sequestered through growth is applied. Any new forest cells appearing in subsequent years will be assigned an age of 3 years (assuming some years of growth have occurred before satellite-based algorithms flagged them as forest pixels).

@section(Use) 
For this service the physical supply = the use as this service benefits the global population regardless of proximity to where the carbon is stored.
